AB  - Background/Objectives: Although approximately 90% of the United States population is now covered by medical insurance; variations in cancer treatment rates and survival persist even among the insured. Medicare offers a uniformly insured cohort where the underlying factors for this variability can be studied. This study evaluates whether nonclinical societal factors are associated with receipt of treatment and survival among Medicare-insured patients with cervical cancer. Methods: This retrospective cohort study used linked SEER-Medicare data from 2000 to 2018 to identify women aged 65 years and older with a first primary diagnosis of uterine cervical cancer. Patients without complete Medicare Parts A and B coverage were excluded. Exposures extracted were race; socioeconomic status (Yost index); and geography relative to healthcare (central metro vs. metro adjacent vs. rural). Outcome measures were primary treatment (surgery; chemotherapy; or radiotherapy within 6 months of diagnosis); cancer-specific survival; and overall survival. Data were analyzed using multivariable regression models. Results: Among 14;552 patients (mean age; 71 years [SD; 12]; 62.8% White; 17.9% Black; 12.0% Hispanic); 11;215 (77.1%) received primary treatment. Higher socioeconomic status was associated with a higher likelihood of receiving treatment (adjusted prevalence ratios [PR] of 1.12; 95% CI; 1.08–1.17 for the highest and 1.09; 95% CI; 1.06–1.14 for the second to highest quintiles respectively) and lower cancer-specific mortality (adjusted hazard ratio [HR]; 0.81; 95% CI; 0.68–0.96 for the highest and 0.83; 95% CI; 0.71–0.97 for the second to highest quintiles respectively). Black patients were less likely to be treated (PR; 0.94; 95% CI; 0.91–0.98); although this did not translate into worse survival rates. Hispanic patients were slightly more likely to receive treatment (PR; 1.03; 95% CI; 1.00–1.06) and showed lower cancer-specific mortality (HR; 0.79; 95% CI; 0.67–0.94). Geography was not associated with treatment in multivariable analysis. Conclusions: In this cohort of Medicare-insured women with cervical cancer; variations in treatment rates and cancer-specific outcomes were associated with socioeconomic status and race; despite health insurance coverage. These findings highlight the need for efforts toward structural interventions to address the social determinants of health.
