AB  - The closure of steel mills along the Monongahela River in the late 20th century dramatically reshaped communities in Allegheny County; Pennsylvania. This case study examines the enduring impacts of deindustrialization on McKeesport; Pennsylvania; one of those river towns and once at the epicenter of the Pittsburgh region's industrial revolution. McKeesport; like many former industrial hubs; has long grappled with the impacts of deindustrialization; including population loss; economic decline; and striking health disparities. The physical landscape bears witness to this transformation; with high rates of blight; vacant land; and abandoned properties. Through the lens of neoliberal capitalism; this research explores how deindustrialization fundamentally altered the community’s fabric and health outcomes. The question guiding this research is how do the long-term outcomes of deindustrialization impacting the built environment contribute to health experiences of the people who live in McKeesport; PA? The study identifies strategies for mitigating these impacts of deindustrialization; particularly by addressing the legacy that vacant land and abandoned property have on health policy implications. Ultimately; this project contributes to the broader discourse on reducing health disparities in deindustrialized communities; offering insights that may inform policy and revitalization efforts in similar post-industrial landscapes across the United States considering persistent neoliberal trends.
