AB  - This paper contributes to research on health disparities among rural and urban residents by considering differences in the magnitude of genetic associations for physical health; mental health; and health behaviors across the two settings. Previous research has shown reduced genetic associations in rural compared to urban settings but none have utilized current genome-wide polygenic scores and none have focused on older adults. Using a sample of 14;994 adults from the 1992 to 2016 waves of the Health and Retirement Study our results suggest genetic associations for BMI (p<.018) and heart conditions (p < .023) are significantly reduced in rural compared to urban settings and we find weak evidence in support of this association for depression (p. < .065) and no evidence for smoking (p < 461). In sum; the weaker genetic associations in rural areas highlights the centrality of the social; economic; and built environment as a determinant of disparities.
