AB  - To examine health inequalities faced by individuals living with mental illness in rural Wyoming and to explore how the Clubhouse model helps address these challenges. This qualitative secondary analysis used semi-structured interview data from a prior study on mental health stigma in rural Wyoming. Sixteen current Clubhouse members ( = 12) and staff ( = 4 ) were recruited from the only Clubhouse in Wyoming between August and September 2024. Data were analyzed using Braun and Clarke's reflexive thematic analysis in NVivo. Two major themes emerged: contributors to health inequalities and the Clubhouse's efforts to address these inequalities. Contributors included limited access to mental health services and structural barriers to essential services; which negatively affected participants' mental health. Clubhouse efforts addressed multiple social determinants of health; including economic stability; health care access and quality; neighborhood and built environment; and social and community context. Individuals living with mental illness in rural communities experience persistent health inequalities that negatively affect their mental health. Participants described the Clubhouse model as a supportive environment that can help address these challenges and promote recovery.
