UR  - https://doi.org/10.1007/s10708-026-11710-8
AB  - Transit-oriented development (TOD) is widely promoted as a template for sustainable; compact and transit-supportive urbanism; yet a growing body of evidence indicates that its benefits are unevenly distributed across urban space and social groups. This study systematically reviews and maps the scientific literature on urban disparities associated with transit-oriented development and transit-centred urban restructuring. Following an adapted PRISMA protocol; 572 records retrieved from Scopus and Web of Science were screened; and 173 English-language journal articles published between 2020 and 2026 were retained for combined bibliometric analysis and thematic synthesis. Performance analysis reveals a rapidly expanding field annual output grew at 28.2% per year between 2020 and 2025 a descriptive within-window statistic rather than a projectable long-run growth parameter; and one that resolves on inspection into a single step change at 2024 concentrated in a Bradford core of eight journals led by the Journal of Transport Geography ; and dominated by first-authored contributions from the United States and China; which together account for 62% of the corpus. Science mapping of keyword co-occurrence identifies three intellectual clusters: (i) accessibility measurement and public transport equity; (ii) spatial analysis of the built environment and ridership disparities; and (iii) health; population and social-outcome disparities. The thematic synthesis distils five cross-cutting dimensions of TOD-related disparity: spatial-accessibility inequity; socio-demographic exclusion; land-value capitalization and gentrification-induced displacement; disparities under system shocks; and the emerging Global South evidence base. The review proposes an integrative equity-assessment framework linking node–place performance to distributional outcomes; and formulates a future research agenda emphasizing causal designs; person-based accessibility; longitudinal displacement tracking; and standardized equity diagnostics for TOD planning practice.
