AB  - The places in which we live strongly influence health behaviors and outcomes. As public health agencies increasingly make local health data accessible to the public; this relationship becomes more apparent. The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention; for example; recently launched the 500 Cities Project; which displays census tract-level estimates of leading health indicators for the 500 largest cities in the United States.1 The Web site's interactive maps reveal clear spatial patterns in rates of various health outcomes; receipt of preventive services; and unhealthy behaviors. A major motivation for providing small-area health data is to encourage public health professionals to leverage this information for program planning; resource allocation; and research initiatives. To identify drivers of geographic differences in health and develop targeted intervention strategies; it is helpful to collect place-based data in communities through primary data collection. Indeed; neighborhood and built environment features (e.g.; healthy food availability; pedestrian infrastructure) are important social determinants of health.2 Field data collection; however; can be time consuming and resource intensive. Although public health professionals continually seek new ways to remotely assess neighborhood characteristics; these methods should not replace the rich and immersive experience of visiting communities in person. New mobile technologies commonly used by industries to collect field data are underused in public health and can improve the efficiency and accuracy of geographic data collection. We outline the advantages of these tools; highlight one example of an economical software application; and present a case study from a project documenting tobacco marketing in New York City neighborhoods.
