AN  - PMID:42763794
AB  - Sleep disturbance and circadian disruption affect 40–60% of women during the menopausal transition; yet public health guidance continues to frame circadian health primarily as a matter of individual behaviour. This commentary argues that such guidance assumes a degree of control over time; light; and rest that many women do not have. Drawing on chronobiology and feminist scholarship on time; we introduce rhythmic agency; the capacity to shape the environmental and temporal conditions that regulate circadian rhythms; as a framework for understanding why women frequently cannot act on behavioural framed advice in everyday life. We extend this with rhythmic justice; which examines how that capacity is unevenly distributed across caregiving roles; occupational demands; and built environments. Together; these concepts reposition circadian disruption during the menopausal transition as a problem of social organisation rather than individual regulation. We close by outlining research directions and policy levers; including work scheduling; nighttime communication norms; urban lighting; and caregiving support; that follow from treating circadian health as a socially distributed capacity.
