AN  - PMID:42864689
AB  - Objective To describe the experience of non‐government organisation (NGO) staff implementing a community‐based stroke recovery program based on the principles of environmental enrichment. Design A qualitative descriptive study to capture staff experiences. Semi‐structured interviews with staff were analysed using reflexive thematic analysis. Setting A community‐based NGO service in regional New South Wales; Australia. Participants Eight staff members employed in a regional NGO and involved in the implementation of the program; including a program coordinator; a physiotherapist; allied health assistants; creative artists and managers. Results Four themes were identified: (1) We've got a great space here—highlighted the importance of the physical environment; access and location; (2) There's an order to it—described the program's strong foundations formed through training; defined processes and support; (3) We make it work—demonstrated the flexibility and adaptability of staff in responding to individual needs and overcoming challenges and (4) Enriching lives—captured the sense of community; positivity and recovery outcomes. Staff experiences aligned closely with self‐determination theory; reflecting feelings of capability; confidence and connection. Conclusion Staff in a regional NGO felt well‐supported and capable of delivering a community‐based stroke program. This demonstrates the potential role NGO services may play in addressing gaps in stroke recovery care in regional Australia. The methods used in this study can be used to implement this and other similar community‐based programs with NGO services in the future. What is Already Known on This Subject? • Community‐based stroke programs can assist recovery after hospital discharge; but access to such programs is limited in rural and regional areas of Australia. • Implementation of multi‐component evidence‐based programs in the community to address this limitation; especially within a regional non‐government organisation (NGO) setting; is under‐explored. What Does This Study Add? • Actively engaging non‐government organisation (NGO) staff in implementation planning helps staff feel well supported to deliver a multi‐component stroke recovery program. • Implementation strategies that support staff competence; autonomy and connectedness; enhance confidence and capability to deliver a multi‐component stroke recovery program. • NGO staff can deliver a multi‐component community‐based stroke recovery program in a regional community.
